@theme lonely
// Writer-commons, themes batch. From content/arcs/outlines.md, "The struggle themes". Any department, either shift.
// Fill-ins assumed: {home} a place ("Lisk"), {family} with "my" ("my gran"), {hobby} an activity ("painting"),
// {since} a length of time ("five years"). A beat that names one lists it in needs:, so people without it skip it.

== beat 1
tone: light
hint: Oh. Hi. You can sit there, if you want. You don't have to.
mem: alone
them: Oh. Hi. I usually eat on my own. It's quiet. I like quiet. Mostly.
them: Everyone's got their table. You find yours, and then you sit at it forever.   [laugh]
? choice
* Mind if I join you?   flavour:practical mem:joined
  them: Oh. No. Yes. I mean, yes, sit. Please.
* Quiet's nice. For a bit.   flavour:share
  them: For a bit. Yes. That's the thing. For a bit.
them: Thanks. This is nice. Don't tell anyone I said nice.

== beat 2
tone: heartfelt
hint: Can I ask you something odd? How do people just... join in?
mem: joinin
them: How do people just join in? Walk up and talk? I stand by the door and then I leave.   [sigh]
them: I'm not shy, exactly. I just never know the moment. Then the moment's gone.
them: It's a busy ship. You can be lonely on a busy ship. That's the silly bit.
? choice
* Come to quiz night with me. You don't have to talk. Just come.   flavour:practical mem:invited
  them: With you? Then I'd have someone to stand next to. Yes. All right.
  + them: Yes. Someone to stand next to. That's all I've ever needed, really. Thank you.
* Not silly. Lots of people feel like that.   flavour:listen mem:notsilly
  them: Do they? They hide it well. Maybe we're all standing by doors.
* I felt like that my first week.   flavour:share mem:firstweek
  them: You? Really? Huh. Then there's hope for me.   [laugh]
them: Thanks. I feel less like the only one now.

== beat 3
tone: resolution
hint: Come and sit with us! Us. I've got an "us" now.
mem: us
them: Sit, sit. Budge up, everyone. This is the one I told you about.   [laugh]
them: I went to quiz night. And again the week after. Now they save me a seat.
them: I've got a table. And it started with you sitting at mine.
? choice
* Told you they'd like you.   flavour:cheer
  them: You did. I didn't believe you. I'm going to start believing you.
* Save me a seat too?   flavour:practical
  them: Always. That's the rule now. There's always a seat for you.
  + them: Always. Right next to me. That one's yours. Permanently. I've told them.
them: Look at me. Talking at a busy table. Who knew.
